{"conjecture": "Long-running systems accumulate implicit invariants—unstated relationships between components that no single change introduces but successive changes preserve through local correctness. Each review and test validates against the explicit contract, while silently widening the graph of assumptions that the next change must satisfy. The system becomes harder to change not because any part is wrong, but because the ratio of implicit to explicit constraints grows until no local view is sufficient to reason about safety."}
